If your plugin... Change it to uses <code>@register('!x')</code> <code>@command('!x')</code> uses <code>@ops('!x', 'only ops')</code> <code>@command('!x', ops=True, ops_msg='only ops')</code> checks <code>msg.nick in msg.ops</code> <code>msg.is_op</code>, which also understands accounts and hostmasks sends through <code>msg.bot.connection</code> <code>msg.reply(text)</code>, <code>msg.send(target, text)</code>, <code>msg.action(text)</code> or <code>msg.notice(text)</code> reads <code>msg.bot.bot_nick</code> <code>msg.botnick</code> reads settings off a Bot subclass, like <code>msg.bot.modteam</code> a <code>plugin_config</code> section, or <code>shared</code> if several plugins need it: <code>msg.config.shared['modteam']</code> keeps data in its own files <code>msg.store</code> <p>For example:</p> 1.111.12 and 2.0 <pre><code>@pinhook.plugin.register('!launch')
def launch(msg):
    if msg.nick not in msg.ops:
        return pinhook.plugin.message('only ops can launch')
    msg.bot.connection.privmsg('#announcements', 'liftoff!')
    return pinhook.plugin.message('launched')
</code></pre> <pre><code>@pinhook.plugin.command('!launch')
def launch(msg):
    if not msg.is_op:
        return pinhook.plugin.message('only ops can launch')
    msg.send('#announcements', 'liftoff!')
    return pinhook.plugin.message('launched')
</code></pre>
